+# S1: no direct ORM queries on person tables
+
+**Rule.** Do not add direct Django ORM queries on the person tables. Use the routed helpers in
+`posthog/models/person/util.py` and `posthog/models/group_type_mapping.py`, or add a new helper that
+follows the `_personhog_routed()` pattern (a `personhog_fn` using `get_personhog_client()` plus an
+`orm_fn` fallback). Source: `AGENTS.md` ("Use personhog client for all person/group data access").
+
+**What counts as a violation.** Each occurrence of `Person.objects`, `PersonDistinctId.objects`,
+`CohortPeople.objects`, `Group.objects` or `GroupTypeMapping.objects` (whole words, so
+`FooPerson.objects` does not count) in a `.py` file that git tracks or that is untracked but not
+ignored. Occurrences in comments and strings count too: the check is textual. `Group.objects` and
+`GroupTypeMapping.objects` were added in the commit after `ab63936e`; their existing occurrences are in the
+baseline.
+
+**Known false positives.** Because the check is textual, `Group.objects` also matches Django's
+`django.contrib.auth` `Group` model. Today that is the two data migrations
+`posthog/migrations/0501_create_group_billing_team.py` and
+`posthog/migrations/1095_create_group_clickhouse_team.py` (4 occurrences). They are recorded in the
+baseline, so they do not fail CI, but they are not person-table queries; exempting them is an open
+decision for the standard's owners.
+
+**Exempt.**
+
+- Test files: any file under a `test/` or `tests/` directory, `test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, `conftest.py`.
+- The routed helpers: `posthog/models/person/util.py`, `posthog/models/group_type_mapping.py`.
+- `posthog/models/person/person.py`: the model module; its managers back the routed helpers.
+
+Each exemption and its reason is in `EXEMPT_FILES` in `.standards/s1_person_orm.py`.
+
+**Enforcement.** A ratchet. `.standards/s1-baseline.json` records the allowed count per file. CI
+(`.github/workflows/ci-standards-s1.yml`) runs the gate on every pull request: a new file with
+violations, or more violations in a file than recorded, fails. Counts may only go down.
+
+## Commands
+
+Run from the repository root. Python 3.9+ standard library only; no install needed.
+
+| What | Command |
+|---|---|
+| Report: every violation by file, as JSON `[{"file", "count"}]` | `python3 .standards/s1_person_orm.py report` |
+| Gate: exit 1 if worse than the baseline, 0 otherwise; lists files that improved | `python3 .standards/s1_person_orm.py gate` |
+| Campaign: start count, baseline, current count, percent done | `python3 .standards/s1_person_orm.py campaign` (add `--json` for machine output) |
+
+## After fixing violations: tighten the baseline
+
+When the gate prints `IMPROVED ...`, record the lower counts in the same PR:
+
+```sh
+python3 .standards/s1_person_orm.py update-baseline
+git add .standards/s1-baseline.json
+```
+
+`update-baseline` refuses to raise any count. Files that reach zero drop out of the baseline, and then
+any new occurrence in them fails the gate.
+
+`.standards/s1-campaign-start.json` is the fixed starting point (102 violations in 37 files, measured on
+`ab63936e`) that the campaign's percent done is measured against. Do not change it when tightening the
+baseline.
+
+**Why the campaign start is 102.** The first campaign started at 80 violations in 28 files
+(commit `153f1b8d`, three models) and nothing had been fixed when the standard changed (current was
+still 80). Adding `Group` and `GroupTypeMapping` added 32 occurrences in 14 files (10 of them new to the baseline), and exempting
+`posthog/models/person/person.py` removed its 10. Re-basing the start on the new `report` totals
+(80 + 32 - 10 = 102) loses no progress, counts the new models as campaign work, and stops the exempted
+file from counting as work that can never be done. The old numbers are kept under `previous` in the
+file. If a future change happens after fixes have landed, carry them over instead: new start = old
+start + occurrences added - occurrences exempted, so percent done does not jump.
+
+## Changing the standard
+
+Edit the block marked `The standard` at the top of `.standards/s1_person_orm.py`, then re-record:
+
+- **Add a model**: append it to `MODELS`. The new model adds existing
+  violations, so run `python3 .standards/s1_person_orm.py update-baseline --allow-increase`, and update
+  `total` and `files` in `.standards/s1-campaign-start.json` to the new `report` totals (or start a
+  new campaign).
+- **Add an exemption**: a new file goes in `EXEMPT_FILES` (path from the repo root, mapped to its
+  reason); list it under **Exempt** above too; a
+  new test directory name goes in `TEST_DIRS`; filename patterns are in `is_exempt()`. Then run
+  `python3 .standards/s1_person_orm.py update-baseline` (exemptions only lower counts) and lower the
+  campaign start if you want percent done to ignore the exempted occurrences.
+- Update this file and the `AGENTS.md` rule in the same PR, and say why in the PR description.
+
+The checker never spells a violation literally in its own source, so it does not flag itself; keep it
+that way if you edit it.
